<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purépecha Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Purépecha, Tarascan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Purépecha Empire, also called the Tarascan state, was a powerful polity in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>. In 1479 it routed an army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a>. This defeat established a western frontier that the Aztecs never crossed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire.jpg" alt="Purépecha Empire" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maunus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarascan_aztec_states.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The kingdom was founded in the early 14th century in the lake basins of the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt, between the Lerma and Balsas rivers. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a>, written around 1540 by the Franciscan Jeronimo de Acalá from the narratives of Purépecha noblemen, a leader named Tariácuri set out to gather the communities around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a> into one state. Around 1300 he made the first conquests and installed his sons Hiripan and Tangáxuan as lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ihuatzio">Ihuatzio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a>, while he ruled from Pátzcuari.</p><p>By Tariácuri's death, around 1350, his lineage, the Vacúxecha or eagles, held every major centre around the lake. Hiripan and later Tangáxuan I extended the conquests to the Lake Cuitzeo area, set up an administrative bureaucracy and divided the tribute of conquered lands among lords and nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under King Tzitzipandáquare some conquests were won and lost again to rebellions or Aztec pressure, but by 1460 the kingdom reached the Pacific coast at Zacatula, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluca-valley">Toluca Valley</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>. After the Aztec defeat of the 1470s the ruler fortified the eastern border with military centres such as Cutzamala and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> and Matlatzinca refugees there on condition that they defend it. From 1480 the Aztec ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a> backed attacks by allied peoples, which King Zuangua repelled. Between 1480 and 1510 the Purépecha occupied parts of Colima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> for their saltpetre mines, until local resistance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">Saltpeter War</a> drove them out.</p><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, King Tangáxuan II exchanged envoys with the Spaniards. In 1522 Cristóbal de Olid reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> and the Purépecha army, perhaps as many as 100,000 men, chose not to fight. Tangáxuan submitted but kept wide autonomy, so that the people paid tribute to him and to Cortés alike. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a>, allied with the noble Don Pedro Panza Cuinierángari, had the king executed on 14 February 1530. In 1543 the land officially became the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The king was called Irecha, a title the Spaniards rendered as Cazonci, and the capital stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a>, the place of hummingbirds, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>. The kingdom included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a>, Matlatzincas, Mazahuas, Chichimec groups and others besides the Purépecha. Its people worshipped many deities, among them Kweráwahpiri, goddess of rain, and Xarhátanha, goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>. The historian Robert West called the Tarascans and their neighbours the foremost metallurgists of pre-Conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>: they worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold and made bronze needles, fishhooks and axeheads.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Purépecha Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's clearest limitation was exposed by the <b>Purépecha</b> (Tarascan) state in the Aztec–Purépecha war of 1479, when Axayacatl's force, reportedly 24,000 strong, was routed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
